 "preamble": "From the answers in each of the 13 rows and 13 columns one letter must be omitted, as often as it occurs, before entry in the diagram (a different letter of the alphabet in each row and column). In the clues the definition parts, and the numbers in brackets, refer to the full unmutilated answers; subsidiary indications (such as anagrams, etc) refer to the mutilated forms to be entered. The omitted letters from the rows (across), taken in order top to bottom, form an appropriate three-word phrase. Using this as the code-phrase for a Playfair word square (see example below) solvers should then be able to decipher the title of the puzzle. Competitors should submit a normal cryptic clue to this title with their entries. In a Playfair word square the codeword or code-phrase (in which no letter recurs) is followed by the remaining letters of the alphabet in order, except Q. To encode a word. It must first be split into pairs of letters, e.g. FI NI SH ED. Each pair is then to be seen as forming the diagonally opposite corners of a rectangle within the word square, the other two corner letters being the coded form. Thus FI gives LM (not ML, which IF would give). When a pair of letters appears in the same row or column, the coded form is produced from the letters immediately to the right of or below each respectively. For the last letters in a row or column the first letters in the same row or column become the coded forms. When all the pairs are coded the word is joined up again, thus: LMUNHORG.\nSHOWJ\nUMPIN\nGABCD\nEFKLR\nTVXYZ",
